if (!_bk) throw new Error("data/canon.js must load before science.js");
(_bk.chapters = _bk.chapters || []).push(
  { n:1, t:"The Right to Be Rich",
    summary:"Whatever may be said in praise of poverty, no one can live a really complete or successful life unless rich: man develops in mind, soul, and body by making use of things, and society is so organized that money is needed to possess them. The object of all life is development, and everything that lives has an inalienable right to all the development it is capable of; man's right to life is his right to the free and unrestricted use of everything necessary to his fullest unfoldment — his right to be rich. Riches are not figurative, and contentment with less is called sinful. Of the three motives of life — body, mind, and soul — none is holier than the others: the body needs food, comfort, and rest; the mind needs books, travel, and beauty; the soul needs love, and love is denied expression by poverty. Desire for riches is desire for abundant life; making the most of yourself is a duty to God and humanity.",
    context:"The book's opening premise: since full life of body, mind, and soul requires things, and money buys things, the right to be rich is the basis of all advancement.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 1", text:"Whatever may be said in praise of poverty, the fact remains that it is not possible to live a really complete or successful life unless one is rich." },
      { ref:"Ch. 1", text:"Man’s right to life means his right to have the free and unrestricted use of all the things which may be necessary to his fullest mental, spiritual, and physical unfoldment; or, in other words, his right to be rich." },
      { ref:"Ch. 1", text:"The desire for riches is really the desire for a richer, fuller, and more abundant life; and that desire is praiseworthy." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Wanting to be rich is right: money is the means by which body, mind, and soul all get the full life they are capable of.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What does Wattles call the basis of all advancement for man?", choices:["The science of getting rich","Renunciation of worldly desires","The favor of religious institutions","Inherited talent and breeding"], a:0, why:"Man develops in mind, soul, and body by making use of things, and society is so organized that he must have money to possess them; therefore the science of getting rich is the basis of all advancement." },
      { q:"By the chapter, why is it wrong to be satisfied with a little?", choices:["Because wanting more violates religious duty","Because no man ought to be satisfied with a little if he is capable of using and enjoying more","Because the poor are punished for laziness","Because only the wealthy can enter public life"], a:1, why:"To be really rich does not mean to be contented with a little; the purpose of Nature is the advancement and unfoldment of life, so every man should have all that contributes to the power, elegance, beauty, and richness of life." },
      { q:"A student believes the noblest life is to renounce money and live only for the soul. Applying the chapter, Wattles would answer that…", choices:["he is right, since the soul outranks body and mind","he is half right, since money matters only for the body","he is right if he also studies the best books","he is wrong, because no one of body, mind, or soul can live fully if the others are cut short"], a:3, why:"The three motives are alike desirable and no one is better or holier than the others; it is not right or noble to live only for the soul and deny mind or body." }
    ] },
  { n:2, t:"There Is a Science of Getting Rich",
    summary:"There is a Science of getting rich, and it is an exact science, like algebra or arithmetic: certain laws govern the process, and anyone who learns and obeys them will get rich with mathematical certainty. The ownership of money and property comes from doing things in a Certain Way, and since like causes always produce like effects, anyone who does things in that Way will infallibly get rich. Wattles argues by elimination: not environment, for rich and poor live side by side in the same neighborhoods and trades; not talent, for many gifted people remain poor while the average get rich; not thrift, for penurious savers are poor while free spenders prosper; nor doing things others fail to do, since two men in the same business do nearly the same things with opposite results. Natural ability is no barrier — anyone who can read and understand these words can get rich. Capital, location, and the right business can be gained along the way; begin in your present business and location.",
    context:"The book's foundational claim, New Thought in classic form: getting rich is a learnable exact science ruled by law, not luck, environment, talent, thrift, or capital.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 2", text:"There is a Science of getting rich, and it is an exact science, like algebra or arithmetic." },
      { ref:"Ch. 2", text:"The ownership of money and property comes as a result of doing things in a certain way; those who do things in this Certain Way, whether on purpose or accidentally, get rich; while those who do not do things in this Certain Way, no matter how hard they work or how able they are, remain poor." },
      { ref:"Ch. 2", text:"No matter how poor you may be, if you begin to do things in the Certain Way you will begin to get rich; and you will begin to have capital." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Getting rich is a learnable law: do things in a Certain Way, and like causes must produce like effects — no matter your start.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What does Wattles say getting rich is an exact science like?", choices:["Algebra or arithmetic","Astronomy or chemistry","Medicine or law","Music or painting"], a:0, why:"He calls it an exact science like algebra or arithmetic, governed by laws that bring riches with mathematical certainty once they are learned and obeyed." },
      { q:"What do two men in the same business and neighborhood — one rich, one poor — prove, by the chapter?", choices:["That environment decides who gets rich","That the rich man had special talent the other lacked","That getting rich is the result of doing things in a Certain Way","That savings, not income, determine wealth"], a:2, why:"Getting rich is not primarily environment, talent, thrift, or doing what others fail to do; the difference between the two men shows the result comes from doing things in a Certain Way." },
      { q:"A broke, debt-ridden reader in a business he dislikes asks where to begin. The chapter's answer is to…", choices:["wait until he has capital to invest","begin in his present business and location to do things in the Certain Way","move to a city where business is booming","abandon business and study a profession"], a:1, why:"Capital, the right business, and the right location can all be gained along the way; he should begin where he is, doing things in the Certain Way which causes success." }
    ] },
  { n:3, t:"Is Opportunity Monopolized?",
    summary:"No man is kept poor because opportunity has been monopolized. Some fields are fenced off — the great railroads — but others are open: the electric railway is in its infancy, and traffic and transportation through the air will soon give employment to millions; turn to that instead of competing with J. J. Hill. The tide of opportunity sets in different directions according to the needs of the Whole; it is setting toward agriculture and allied trades, so the farmer and those who supply him find the open door. Workers are not kept down by trusts: as a class they remain where they are because they do not do things in a Certain Way, and the individual worker can follow the tide now. Nor is anyone kept poor by a shortness of supply — from America's building material alone a palace might be built for every family on earth. Everything is made from one Original Substance, the Formless Stuff, whose supply is inexhaustible, intelligent, and always impelled toward more life.",
    context:"The answer to the monopoly objection: opportunity and raw supply are inexhaustible, because all things are forms of one Formless Substance that is alive and increasing.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 3", text:"No man is kept poor because opportunity has been taken away from him; because other people have monopolized the wealth, and have put a fence around it." },
      { ref:"Ch. 3", text:"Everything you see on earth is made from one original substance, out of which all things proceed." },
      { ref:"Ch. 3", text:"Nature is an inexhaustible storehouse of riches; the supply will never run short." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Opportunity is not monopolized: the supply of Formless Substance is inexhaustible, and the man who moves with the tide of need will find openings.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What does Wattles predict will become a great industry giving employment to millions?", choices:["Steam railway expansion","Deep-sea mining","Traffic and transportation through the air","Telephone communication"], a:2, why:"He tells readers to turn from the monopolized steam railways to aerial transportation, which will give employment to hundreds of thousands, and perhaps to millions." },
      { q:"Why does Wattles argue that no man is poor because nature is poor?", choices:["Because gold mines have barely been tapped","Because everything is made from one Original Substance whose supply is inexhaustible","Because governments can print all the money needed","Because charity redistributes the surplus"], a:1, why:"All forms are shapes of one Thing; the visible supply is practically inexhaustible and the invisible supply really IS inexhaustible, so there is more than enough for all." },
      { q:"A factory worker feels the trusts have kept him down. The chapter's counsel is to…", choices:["organize a strike against the trust","wait for laws to break the trusts","accept that his class has determined his fate","follow the tide of opportunity and begin doing things in a Certain Way"], a:3, why:"The worker is not held down by the ignorance or mental slothfulness of his class; individually he can follow the tide of opportunity to riches by learning the Certain Way." }
    ] },
  { n:4, t:"The First Principle in the Science of Getting Rich",
    summary:"Thought is the only power which can produce tangible riches from Formless Substance, for the stuff from which all things are made is a substance which thinks, and a thought of form in it produces the form, generally along lines of growth already established. Man is a thinking center capable of original thought, yet so far he has only reshaped existing forms by manual labor. The chapter lays down three fundamental propositions: there is a thinking stuff which permeates, penetrates, and fills the interspaces of the universe; a thought in this substance produces the thing imaged by the thought; and man, by impressing his thought upon formless substance, can cause the thing he thinks about to be created. Take these on trust and dwell on them until habitual, for a man's way of doing things results from the way he thinks: to think truth regardless of appearances — health amid disease, riches amid poverty — is the hardest work in the world, and whoever acquires it becomes a MASTER MIND.",
    context:"The book's monistic creed, stated and repeated: thought impressed on the thinking stuff creates form, and man must learn to think truth regardless of appearances.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 4", text:"Thought is the only power which can produce tangible riches from the Formless Substance." },
      { ref:"Ch. 4", text:"There is a thinking stuff from which all things are made, and which, in its original state, permeates, penetrates, and fills the interspaces of the universe." },
      { ref:"Ch. 4", text:"To think what you want to think is to think TRUTH, regardless of appearances." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Think truth regardless of appearances: hold to abundance amid poverty. Sustained thought is the hardest work in the world — and the first step toward riches.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What does the chapter name as the only power that can produce tangible riches from Formless Substance?", choices:["Sustained manual labor","Thought","Accumulated capital","Natural talent"], a:1, why:"The chapter opens with the claim that thought is the only power which can produce tangible riches, because the stuff from which all things are made is a substance which thinks." },
      { q:"What happens, by the chapter, when a thought of form is held in thinking Substance?", choices:["It fades unless repeated aloud daily","It only changes the thinker's mood","It causes the creation of the form, generally along lines of growth already established","It must first be approved by other minds"], a:2, why:"Every thought of form held in thinking Substance causes the creation of the form, but always, or at least generally, along lines of growth and action already established." },
      { q:"A man surrounded by visible poverty wants to follow the science. The chapter instructs him to…", choices:["hold to the truth that there is no poverty, only abundance","study poverty until he understands its causes","avoid the subject of money altogether","imitate the habits of rich neighbors"], a:0, why:"To think riches in the midst of appearances of poverty requires power, acquired by holding the basic fact of the one Thinking Substance; he who acquires it becomes a MASTER MIND." }
    ] },
  { n:5, t:"Increasing Life",
    summary:"You must get rid of the last vestige of the old idea of a Deity who wants you poor. The Intelligent Substance is consciously alive and, like every living thing, under the necessity of increase: a seed living produces a hundred more seeds; every thought leads to another; every cultivated talent awakens the desire for more. To know more, do more, and be more we must have more things to use, so the desire for riches is the capacity for larger life seeking fulfillment, and it is God's desire that you get rich, because he can express himself better through you when you have plenty. Your purpose must harmonize with the purpose in All: real life, not swinish pleasure, not intellect alone, and not extreme altruism, which is no nobler than extreme selfishness — you help others most by making the most of yourself. And Substance works for more life to all: drop the competitive thought, create rather than compete, for competitive riches are never satisfactory or permanent.",
    context:"Why riches are legitimate in a 1910 New Thought frame: life's inherent urge to become more makes wealth the means of increase, on the creative plane only.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 5", text:"The desire for riches is simply the capacity for larger life seeking fulfillment; every desire is the effort of an unexpressed possibility to come into action." },
      { ref:"Ch. 5", text:"But remember that extreme altruism is no better and no nobler than extreme selfishness; both are mistakes." },
      { ref:"Ch. 5", text:"Riches secured on the competitive plane are never satisfactory and permanent; they are yours to-day, and another’s to-morrow." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Get rich by creation, not competition: Substance works for more life to all, never by taking from another, so never think the supply limited.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Why does the chapter say it is God's desire that you should get rich?", choices:["So you can endow churches and charities","Because he can express himself better through you if you have plenty of things to use","Because wealth proves divine election","Because poverty is a sin against family"], a:1, why:"The One Substance wants to live more in you; he wants you rich because he can express himself better through you if you have unlimited command of the means of life." },
      { q:"What does the chapter say happens the moment you begin to think the supply is being cornered and limited?", choices:["Your power to cause creation is gone for the time being","Your riches are transferred to the bankers","Your desire weakens but your faith holds","Nothing, so long as you keep working"], a:0, why:"In that moment you drop into the competitive mind, your power to cause creation is gone, and you will probably arrest the creative movements you have already instituted." },
      { q:"A man is tempted to win a contract by ruining a rival. On the chapter's terms, that temptation means…", choices:["acting on the creative plane, since winning is natural","following the example of Rockefeller and Carnegie in their best work","safe, because business has its own rules","dropping into the competitive mind, seeking riches that cannot be satisfactory or permanent"], a:3, why:"To get rich in a scientific and certain way you must rise entirely out of the competitive thought; riches secured on the competitive plane are never satisfactory and permanent." }
    ] },
  { n:6, t:"How Riches Come to You",
    summary:"Not driving sharp bargains does not mean driving none: it means dealing fairly, giving every man more in use value than you take from him in cash value. The book's paper and ink may be worth less than its price, yet if its ideas bring you thousands it gave great use value for small cash value; a painting sold to an Eskimo for $500 in furs wrongs him because it adds nothing to his life, while a $50 gun that gets him many more furs makes him rich. If your business beats people, get out of it at once. Riches need not take shape before your eyes: hold the mental image of the sewing machine with unquestioning faith, and the Supreme Intelligence, acting on the minds of men, brings it through transactions as much to the other man's advantage as yours. The desire you feel for riches is the Infinite seeking expression, like the boy at the piano who felt music he could not yet play — so ask largely.",
    context:"How creation arrives: through fair exchange — more use value than cash taken — and through ordinary commerce, delivered to the man of vision and faith.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 6", text:"Give every man more in use value than you take from him in cash value; then you are adding to the life of the world by every business transaction." },
      { ref:"Ch. 6", text:"You need not hesitate about asking largely; “it is your Father’s pleasure to give you the kingdom,” said Jesus." },
      { ref:"Ch. 6", text:"The desire you feel for riches is the Infinite, seeking to express Himself in you as He sought to find expression in the little boy at the piano." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Give every man more in use value than the cash you take from him, ask largely, and hold your pictured desire as already on its way.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"In the chapter's example, why does selling an Eskimo a great painting for $500 in furs wrong him?", choices:["The price was far below the painting's worth","The picture had no use value for him, so it added nothing to his life","Furs were needed by the seller himself","The Eskimo could not legally own art"], a:1, why:"He has no use for the picture, so it will not add to his life; but a gun worth $50 will get him many more furs and much food, so it makes him rich." },
      { q:"How does Wattles say a sewing machine you have visioned will actually reach you?", choices:["It will materialize in your room without hands","Through charity from a wealthy stranger","Through the power of the Supreme Intelligence acting on the minds of men, in ordinary transactions","By winning it in a contest of faith"], a:2, why:"You hold the mental image with unquestioning faith that it is coming, and it is brought by the Supreme acting upon the minds of men — the whole matter being as much to that man's advantage as to yours." },
      { q:"A shopkeeper can double his margin on a tool that will barely serve his customer. On the creative plane, by the chapter, he…", choices:["may proceed, since the buyer agreed freely","must sell it at a loss to show faith","should sell only to rich customers","must stop, because the sale adds less to the customer's life than the cash it takes"], a:3, why:"If you are selling any man anything which does not add more to his life than the thing he gives you in exchange, you can afford to stop it." }
    ] },
  { n:7, t:"Gratitude",
    summary:"The first step toward getting rich is conveying the idea of your wants to Formless Substance, and that requires relating yourself to the Formless Intelligence in a harmonious way summed up in one word: gratitude. You believe there is one Intelligent Substance from which all things proceed, believe that it gives you what you desire, and relate yourself to it by deep and profound gratitude; many rightly ordered lives are kept poor by the lack of it. There is a Law of Gratitude: action and reaction are always equal and in opposite directions, so the grateful outreach of the mind cannot fail to reach the Supreme, and the reaction is an instantaneous movement toward you. Gratitude also fixes the mind on the best — dwelling on the squalid and mean makes the mind take their form and brings such things to you — and it is the birth of faith. Give thanks continuously for everything, including even plutocrats and politicians, whose organization of the world made your opportunity.",
    context:"The mental-adjustment chapter: gratitude is the attitude that unifies the mind with creative Substance, keeps thought off the limited supply, and fixes it on the best.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 7", text:"There is a Law of Gratitude, and it is absolutely necessary that you should observe the law, if you are to get the results you seek." },
      { ref:"Ch. 7", text:"“Draw nigh unto God, and He will draw nigh unto you.” That is a statement of psychological truth." },
      { ref:"Ch. 7", text:"The grateful mind is constantly fixed upon the best; therefore it tends to become the best; it takes the form or character of the best, and will receive the best." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Give thanks continuously: gratitude keeps you in touch with the source of blessings, off the thought of limited supply, and fixed on the best.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"In one word, what does the chapter say sums up the whole process of mental adjustment and atonement?", choices:["Faith","Gratitude","Prayer","Desire"], a:1, why:"The whole process of mental adjustment and atonement is summed up in one word — gratitude — the feeling that relates you to the Formless Intelligence in a harmonious way." },
      { q:"What, by the chapter, happens to a mind that dwells with dissatisfaction on the poor and squalid?", choices:["It takes the form of those things and transmits them, so the poor and squalid come to it","It becomes more determined to fight poverty","It is protected by its realistic outlook","It loses nothing but a little time"], a:0, why:"To permit your mind to dwell upon the inferior is to become inferior and surround yourself with inferior things; thinking substance takes the form of that which it thinks about." },
      { q:"Bad news about magnates and politicians tempts a reader into angry talk. The chapter's counsel is to…", choices:["study their methods to defeat them","stay silent until laws change them","be grateful to them, since their organization has made your opportunity","write protests to the newspapers"], a:2, why:"Their organization of the world has made your opportunity; all you get really comes to you because of them, so remember that they are all very good and be grateful to them all." }
    ] },
  { n:8, t:"Thinking in the Certain Way",
    summary:"You must form a clear and definite mental picture of what you want: you cannot transmit an idea you do not have, and vague longings never start creative power — as a wireless message must be a coherent sentence, desires must be arranged into a coherent whole. Hold the picture continually in mind, as the sailor holds the port he is sailing toward; no special concentration exercises are needed, only desire strong enough to keep it in your thoughts. But vision alone makes only a dreamer: behind it must stand the purpose to realize it, and behind that, unwavering faith that the thing is already yours. Dwell on the picture until it is distinct, take the Mental Attitude of Ownership, and be as thankful now as when it takes form. The answer to prayer is according to your faith while you are working, not while talking: to pray without ceasing is to hold the vision with purpose and faith — the difference between the scientist and the dreamer.",
    context:"The visualization method made precise: a definite picture held with purpose, unwavering faith, mental ownership, and gratitude now — prayer as working faith, not repetition.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 8", text:"You must form a clear and definite mental picture of what you want; you cannot transmit an idea unless you have it yourself." },
      { ref:"Ch. 8", text:"“Whatsoever things ye ask for when ye pray, believe that ye receive them, and ye shall have them,” said Jesus." },
      { ref:"Ch. 8", text:"Remember that it is faith and purpose in the use of the imagination which make the difference between the scientist and the dreamer." }
    ],
    takeaway:"See exactly what you want, hold it with purpose and unwavering faith, take mental ownership, and give thanks as if it were already yours.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What image does the chapter use for how you should hold your mental picture?", choices:["A sailor holding the port toward which he is sailing","A miner guarding a claim","A judge weighing evidence","A merchant counting stock"], a:0, why:"You must keep the clear mental picture continually in mind, as the sailor has in mind the port toward which he is sailing the ship, and no more lose sight of it than the steersman loses sight of the compass." },
      { q:"Why does the chapter say vision alone is not enough?", choices:["Because pictures cannot cross distance","Because without purpose and faith behind it you are only a dreamer with little or no power for accomplishment","Because visions must be kept secret to work","Because the picture must change weekly to stay fresh"], a:1, why:"If merely seeing the picture is all you do, you are only a dreamer; behind the clear vision must be the purpose to realize it and unwavering FAITH that the thing is already yours." },
      { q:"A woman repeats her written desire each morning, then forgets it all day. By the chapter…", choices:["her method is complete as taught","she should add fasting to strengthen it","repetition alone cannot impress Substance; she must hold the vision with faith and purpose while she works","she should keep the card secret from others"], a:2, why:"The answer to prayer is not according to your faith while you are talking, but according to your faith while you are working; a set hour of prayer with the matter dismissed the rest of the day cannot impress the mind of God." }
    ] },
  { n:9, t:"How to Use the Will",
    summary:"To get rich in a scientific way you do not apply your will to anything outside yourself. It is wrong to coerce people by mental power — as much slavery as physical force — and you have no right to use your will on another even for his own good, since you do not know what that is; nor need you compel things or God, for Substance is friendly and more anxious to give than you are to receive. The legitimate use of the will is upon yourself: to hold yourself to the right course, keep your mind at home, and hold the vision with faith and purpose. Steady faith makes only positive impressions, while every hour of doubt, worry, and unbelief sets a current away from you. Since beliefs are shaped by what you attend to, command your attention: do not study poverty — health is never won by studying disease, nor righteousness by studying sin. The poor need inspiration, not charity; get rich, and demonstrate the way.",
    context:"The ethics of mental power: the will is never projected at people or things, but turned on oneself to hold attention away from poverty and on the vision.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 9", text:"It is wrong to apply your will to other men and women, in order to get them to do what you wish done." },
      { ref:"Ch. 9", text:"To get rich, you need only to use your will power upon yourself." },
      { ref:"Ch. 9", text:"Use your will power to keep your mind OFF the subject of poverty, and to keep it fixed with faith and purpose ON the vision of what you want." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Never project your will on people or things; use it on yourself alone — to keep your mind off poverty and fixed, with faith and purpose, on your vision.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"By the chapter, upon what is the only legitimate use of the will in getting rich?", choices:["Upon market prices","Upon yourself","Upon your competitors","Upon Formless Substance"], a:1, why:"When you know what to think and do, you must use your will to compel yourself to think and do the right things — to hold yourself to the right course — keeping your mind at home." },
      { q:"Why does the chapter forbid using will power on another person even for his own good?", choices:["Because it weakens the will of the user","Because mental effort is tiring and wasteful","Because you do not know what is for his good, and coercion by mental means is slavery as much as by physical force","Because other people's minds are out of reach"], a:2, why:"Compelling people by mental power accomplishes exactly what physical force does — reduces them to slavery and is robbery in principle; and you do not know what is for another's good." },
      { q:"A reader wants to help the poor and plans to devote himself to charity work and studying slum conditions. Applying the chapter…", choices:["he has found the surest path to reform","he should first master the causes of poverty","he should get rich himself, since the poor need inspiration, not charity","he should distribute literature about wretchedness"], a:0, why:"Charity only tends to perpetuate the wretchedness it aims to eradicate; what helps the poor is getting pictures of wealth into their minds, demonstrated by getting rich yourself." }
    ] },
  { n:10, t:"Further Use of the Will",
    summary:"You cannot retain a true and clear vision of wealth while turning your attention to opposing pictures. Do not tell of past financial troubles or your parents' poverty; let the dead bury their dead. Give up doomsday religion and the muck-rakers' pessimism: the world is not going to the devil, it is going to God — a wonderful Becoming. Interest yourself in the world's becoming rich, speak of the poor as becoming rich, and remember that on the competitive plane the struggle for riches is a Godless scramble for power over other men, while in the creative mind health, greatness, love, and soul unfoldment all come by way of getting rich — the noblest aim because it includes everything else. Wattles then narrows the shelf: this book gives the whole science as mathematics needs only four operations, so read it daily, lay other systems aside, postpone occult studies, and read only the most optimistic news. He closes by restating the creed with its demand for fixed purpose and unwavering faith.",
    context:"Guarding the mind's inputs: ignore poverty, pessimism, rival systems, and the occult; attend only to riches through the creative method and this book alone.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 10", text:"The world is not going to the devil; it is going to God." },
      { ref:"Ch. 10", text:"To become really rich is the noblest aim you can have in life, for it includes everything else." },
      { ref:"Ch. 10", text:"The very best thing you can do for the whole world is to make the most of yourself." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Put poverty and its pictures behind you: think of the world's becoming rich, read only what harmonizes with your vision, and make the most of yourself.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What does the chapter tell readers to do about past financial troubles?", choices:["Tell the story often to warn others","Set aside one day a year to review them","Not think of them at all, and put poverty behind them","Analyze them until the lesson is learned"], a:2, why:"To tell or think of past financial troubles is to mentally class yourself with the poor for the time being, and it will certainly check the movement of things in your direction." },
      { q:"Why does the chapter say to give heed to no conflicting theories or other systems?", choices:["Because all rival authors are dishonest","Because doubt and wavering thought will begin, and then you will begin to make failures","Because the systems contradict the laws of nature","Because reading widely is a waste of money"], a:1, why:"If you think about other systems you will begin to have doubts and be uncertain and wavering in your thought, and then you will begin to make failures." },
      { q:"A student's friends urge him to attend lectures teaching a different mental system. By the chapter he should…", choices:["refuse, lay all others aside, and keep to this book until he has gained what he wants","attend and blend the best of both","go, but only to debate the speakers","ask the lecturers to prove their claims"], a:0, why:"When you commence on this, lay all others aside; mixing systems starts mental cross-currents which will surely bring your hopes to shipwreck." }
    ] },
  { n:11, t:"Acting in the Certain Way",
    summary:"Thought is the creative power, but you must not rely on thought alone — that is the rock on which many metaphysical thinkers shipwreck, the failure to connect thought with personal action. Gold impelled toward you by thought will not mine itself and roll into your pocket: you must arrange your affairs to receive what comes, giving every man more in use value than he gives you in cash value. By thought the thing you want is brought to you; by action you receive it. Action must be now, in the present: you cannot act in the past or future, and action with the mind on the future is a divided mind, ineffective. So put your whole mind into present action, in your present business and environment — do today's work well — holding the vision of the better place while using the present one as the means of reaching it; the employee holds the vision of the job he wants while acting on the job he has.",
    context:"The hinge from thought to deed: personal action, taken now in one's present business, is what receives what the vision brings.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 11", text:"By thought, the thing you want is brought to you; by action you receive it." },
      { ref:"Ch. 11", text:"You cannot act where you are not; you cannot act where you have been, and you cannot act where you are going to be; you can act only where you are." },
      { ref:"Ch. 11", text:"Hold the vision of yourself in the job you want, while you ACT with faith and purpose on the job you have, and you will certainly get the job you want." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Do not rely on thought alone: by thought the thing is brought to you, but only by acting now, in your present place and business, can you receive it.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What does the chapter call the rock upon which many otherwise scientific metaphysical thinkers meet shipwreck?", choices:["The failure to connect thought with personal action","Praying for the wrong objects","Trusting the guidance of other people","Setting the vision too high"], a:0, why:"Thinking in a Certain Way will bring riches, but many fail by relying upon thought alone and paying no attention to personal action." },
      { q:"Why must action always be in the present, by the chapter's reasoning?", choices:["Because the future can be controlled only by prophets","Because you cannot act in the past or the not-yet-arrived future, and divided-mind action is ineffective","Because present action earns compound interest","Because the past holds mistakes that must be corrected first"], a:1, why:"You cannot act in the past or the future; if you act in the present with your mind on the future, your present action will be with a divided mind and will not be effective." },
      { q:"An employee dreams of a better job and spends her evenings picturing it. To complete the Certain Way she must…", choices:["send out her thought to influence the hiring manager","quit immediately to force the issue","wait until the new job appears before acting","act with faith and purpose on the job she has while holding the vision of the job she wants"], a:3, why:"Hold the vision of yourself in the job you want while you act with faith and purpose on the job you have, and you will certainly get the job you want." }
    ] },
  { n:12, t:"Efficient Action",
    summary:"Do what you can do where you are — and do ALL that you can do. You can advance only by being larger than your present place; the world is advanced only by those who more than fill their places, just as evolution is caused by excess of life. Every day is either a successful day or a failure, and every act is in itself either efficient or inefficient: the cause of failure is doing too many things in an inefficient manner and not doing enough in an efficient one. It is not the number of things you do but the efficiency of each separate action that counts, and any act can be made strong by holding your vision while you do it and putting the whole power of your faith and purpose into it. Successful action is cumulative: do each day's work fully, without overwork or haste, and contemplate the vision's details in leisure hours until it is fixed.",
    context:"The efficiency principle: each day's acts done fully and each act done with faith and purpose is the mechanism by which vision becomes riches.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 12", text:"It is really not the number of things you do, but the EFFICIENCY of each separate action that counts." },
      { ref:"Ch. 12", text:"The cause of failure is doing too many things in an inefficient manner, and not doing enough things in an efficient manner." },
      { ref:"Ch. 12", text:"Every act can be made strong and efficient by holding your vision while you are doing it, and putting the whole power of your FAITH and PURPOSE into it." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Do every day all that can be done that day, and make each act efficient by holding your vision and putting the whole power of faith and purpose into it.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"By the chapter, what — rather than the number of things you do — is what counts?", choices:["The speed of each action","The efficiency of each separate action","The variety of your tasks","The approval of your employer"], a:1, why:"It is really not the number of things you do but the EFFICIENCY of each separate action that counts; every inefficient act is a failure in itself." },
      { q:"How does the chapter say any ordinary act can be made a success in itself?", choices:["By holding your vision while doing it and putting the whole power of your faith and purpose into it","By doing it faster than anyone else","By doing it for the sake of your employer's approval","By alternating it with periods of rest"], a:0, why:"Every act can be made strong and efficient by holding your vision while you are doing it and putting the whole power of your FAITH and PURPOSE into it, since All Power is working with you and cannot fail." },
      { q:"A man works fourteen-hour days at rushed, scattered tasks and still fails. The chapter's diagnosis is…", choices:["he lacks talent for his trade","his business is wrong and must be changed at once","he is doing too many things in an inefficient manner and not enough in an efficient manner","he has not prayed often enough"], a:2, why:"The cause of failure is doing too many things in an inefficient manner and not doing enough things in an efficient manner; you are not to overwork or rush blindly." }
    ] },
  { n:13, t:"Getting into the Right Business",
    summary:"Success in a particular business depends partly on well-developed faculties, but talent does not insure riches — skilled musicians, blacksmiths, and merchants still fail — because faculties are tools, and good tools must be used in the Right Way. You will do best in the business using your strongest faculties, yet no vocation is fixed by birth: any rudimentary talent can be developed, making your tools as you go. You get rich most easily doing what you are best fitted for and most satisfactorily doing what you want to do; desire is the proof of the power, for strong desire to do a thing proves the power to do it, needing only development. Change by growth rather than haste, never acting suddenly while in doubt; there is never any hurry on the creative plane and no lack of opportunity. In doubt, contemplate your vision and cultivate gratitude — and never hurry, for the moment you hurry you cease to be a creator and become a competitor.",
    context:"Vocation counsel within the system: choose work that fits talent and desire, change by growth without haste, and steady doubt with gratitude.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 13", text:"The various faculties of your mind are the tools with which you must do the work which is to make you rich; it will be easier for you to succeed if you get into a business for which you are well equipped with mental tools." },
      { ref:"Ch. 13", text:"You will get rich most easily in point of effort, if you do that for which you are best fitted; but you will get rich most satisfactorily if you do that which you WANT to do." },
      { ref:"Ch. 13", text:"Remember that in the moment you begin to hurry you cease to be a creator and become a competitor; you drop back upon the old plane again." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Follow the work your desire points to — desire is proof of power — and change by growth, never in haste: the moment you hurry you become a competitor.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What does the chapter say a strong desire to do a thing is proof of?", choices:["That you are wasting your time","That the power which can do it is strong within you and needs only development","That you should imitate someone successful","That the business will be profitable"], a:1, why:"Where there is strong desire to do a thing it is certain proof that the power to do it is strong, and only requires to be developed and applied in the Right Way." },
      { q:"Why does talent alone not insure getting rich, by the chapter's tool analogy?", choices:["Because talentless people always outcompete the gifted","Because markets reward luck more than skill","Because faculties are tools, and good tools must also be used in the Right Way","Because talent fades without constant practice"], a:2, why:"One man with a sharp saw, a square, and a good plane builds handsome furniture while another with the same tools produces a botch; the faculties must be used in the successful way." },
      { q:"A reader feels misplaced but is tempted to quit this week on impulse. The chapter advises him to…", choices:["quit at once; the universe rewards boldness","stay forever, since change is always wrong","ask the employer to decide for him","change by growth, and never take radical action while in doubt"], a:3, why:"The best way to change business or environment is by growth; never take sudden or radical action when you are in doubt, for there is never any hurry on the creative plane." }
    ] },
  { n:14, t:"The Impression of Increase",
    summary:"Whether or not you change vocations, the key-thought of all your dealings must be to convey to every mind the impression of increase. Increase is what all men and women are seeking — the urge of the Formless Intelligence within them — and because it is the deepest instinct of their natures, people are attracted to whoever can give them more of the means of life. So put the thought of increase into every transaction, even selling a stick of candy, and let your unshakable faith that you are advancing, and advancing all who deal with you, permeate every act and tone and look — without boasting, for true faith is never boastful, and the boastful are secretly doubtful and afraid. Give a use value greater than the cash value you take, and people will go where they are given increase. But beware the insidious temptation to seek power over other men: the mind that seeks mastery is the competitive mind, not the creative one.",
    context:"The social face of the Certain Way: every transaction carries the impression of increase, while the competitive lust for power over others is refused.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 14", text:"Increase is what all men and all women are seeking; it is the urge of the Formless Intelligence within them, seeking fuller expression." },
      { ref:"Ch. 14", text:"Convey the impression of advancement with everything you do, so that all people shall receive the impression that you are an Advancing Man, and that you advance all who deal with you." },
      { ref:"Ch. 14", text:"“What I want for myself, I want for everybody.”" }
    ],
    takeaway:"Let every act and tone convey quiet assurance of increase — you are advancing, and all who deal with you advance too; never seek power over others.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What does the chapter name as the key-thought of all your efforts in dealing with other men?", choices:["Conveying to their minds the impression of increase","Getting the lowest possible price","Keeping your plans strictly secret","Winning their personal friendship"], a:0, why:"In so far as your business consists in dealing with other men, whether personally or by letter, the key-thought of all your efforts must be to convey to their minds the impression of increase." },
      { q:"Why does the chapter warn against boasting of your success?", choices:["Boasting offends wealthy customers","Words are unnecessary and dangerous in business","Wherever you find a boastful person you find one who is secretly doubtful and afraid","Boasting invites taxation and scrutiny"], a:2, why:"True faith is never boastful; the boastful are secretly doubtful and afraid — let every act and tone express quiet assurance, and words will not be necessary." },
      { q:"A merchant wants loyal customers and steady growth. Applying the chapter, his daily rule is to…", choices:["undercut every rival's prices","make each customer feel that dealing with him brings increase","advertise more heavily than competitors","expand into new towns each year"], a:1, why:"People will go where they are given increase; see that you give them a use value greater than the cash value you take, and the Supreme will move customers toward you." }
    ] },
  { n:15, t:"The Advancing Man",
    summary:"What was said of merchants applies to physicians, teachers, clergymen, and wage-earners alike. The physician who holds the vision of himself as a great and successful healer, working toward it with faith and purpose, comes into such touch with the Source of Life that patients come in throngs; the clergyman who can teach and demonstrate the science of abundant life will never lack a congregation; the teacher who inspires children with the advancing life will never be out of a job — the law of the increase of life is as mathematically certain as gravitation. The wage-earner should form the clear vision and do every piece of work in a perfectly successful manner — but not to curry favor, for the employer profits by keeping a merely good workman where he is. The certain to advance are those too big for their place and determined to be what they want to be; opportunities will speedily come, for God cannot help helping the man who acts in a Certain Way.",
    context:"The method extended to every station: physician, clergyman, teacher, and wage-earner advance by outgrowing the present place, not by pleasing an employer.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 15", text:"The law of the Increase of Life is as mathematically certain in its operation as the law of gravitation; getting rich is an exact science." },
      { ref:"Ch. 15", text:"The man who is certain to advance is the one who is too big for his place, and who has a clear concept of what he wants to be; who knows that he can become what he wants to be, and who is determined to BE what he wants to be." },
      { ref:"Ch. 15", text:"Do not wait for an opportunity to be all that you want to be; when an opportunity to be more than you are now is presented and you feel impelled toward it, take it." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Work each task perfectly — for your own advancement, not your employer's favor — and opportunities will speedily come to the Advancing Man.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Why does the chapter say the merely good workman, satisfied with his place, is unlikely to be promoted?", choices:["Employers reward only seniority","He is worth more to the employer where he is","Promotions depend on luck and timing","Employers fear ambitious men"], a:1, why:"A man who is merely a good workman, filling his place to the very best of his ability and satisfied with that, is valuable where he is, and it is not to the employer's interest to promote him." },
      { q:"With what motive should a wage-earner more than fill his present place, by the chapter?", choices:["To shame slower coworkers","To gain the foreman's personal friendship","With the idea of advancing himself, holding the faith and purpose of increase in and out of work hours","To qualify for a raise as quickly as possible"], a:2, why:"Do not try to more than fill your present place with a view to pleasing your employer; do it with the idea of advancing yourself, holding the faith and purpose of increase at all times." },
      { q:"A clerk sees no path up in her firm. Applying the chapter, she should…", choices:["begin thinking and acting in the Certain Way, doing each piece of work in a perfectly successful manner, and watch for the opportunity that follows","petition the owners for a new post","accept that the industrial situation has fixed her place","change firms repeatedly until one promotes her"], a:0, why:"There is nothing in her circumstances or the industrial situation that can keep her down; faith and purpose will make her quick to see, and bring, opportunities to better her condition." }
    ] },
  { n:16, t:"Some Cautions, and Concluding Observations",
    summary:"Many will scoff that getting rich can be an exact science, insisting institutions must change first; but individuals may enter upon the Certain Way under any government, and when enough do, systems modify themselves to open the way for others — the more who get rich on the creative plane, the better for all. Hold thought on the creative plane: never regard the supply as limited. Do not plan anxiously for future emergencies or looming obstacles; go on in the Certain Way and they disappear or open a way around, for no combination of circumstances can defeat the man or woman who obeys the law, any more than one can multiply two by two and fail to get four. Guard your speech: never speak discouragedly, never admit the possibility of failure, never call the times hard. And never feel disappointed: seeming failure means you have not asked for enough, as in the case of the student whose failed business deal made room for a far better one.",
    context:"Closing cautions: no government or system blocks the Advancing Mind; guard speech, refuse fear, and read every apparent failure as a request too small.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 16", text:"No man or woman who obeys the law can fail to get rich, any more than one can multiply two by two and fail to get four." },
      { ref:"Ch. 16", text:"When you make a failure, it is because you have not asked for enough; keep on, and a larger thing than you were seeking will certainly come to you." },
      { ref:"Ch. 16", text:"Guard your speech. Never speak of yourself, your affairs, or of anything else in a discouraged or discouraging way." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Guard your speech, expect no disappointment: a seeming failure means you have not asked for enough — keep faith, keep grateful, and a larger thing will come.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"By the chapter, what does a real failure mean?", choices:["That the law has exceptions","That you have not asked for enough","That your talent is still undeveloped","That the times are against you"], a:1, why:"When you make a failure it is because you have not asked for enough; keep on, and a larger thing than you were seeking will certainly come to you." },
      { q:"Why does the chapter say to give no anxious thought to possible disasters or panics?", choices:["Because planning is useless in all cases","Because disasters strike only the pessimistic","Because every difficulty carries with it the wherewithal for its overcoming, and obstacles disappear or open a way as you approach","Because the government will prevent them"], a:2, why:"Meet such things only when they present themselves; go on in the Certain Way and the obstruction will disappear as you approach it, or a way over, through, or around it will appear." },
      { q:"A hoped-for business deal collapses at the last moment. The chapter's follower will…", choices:["treat it as proof the science failed","suspect rivals of secret interference","abandon the vision and start another","thank God it was overruled and go on gratefully, expecting a greater good"], a:3, why:"The student in the chapter thanked God that his desire had been overruled, and weeks later an opportunity so much better came that he would not have made the first deal on any account." }
    ] },
  { n:17, t:"Summary of the Science of Getting Rich",
    summary:"The closing chapter states the whole science on one page. There is a thinking stuff from which all things are made, filling the interspaces of the universe; a thought in it produces the thing imaged; and man, by impressing his thought upon formless substance, can cause the thing he thinks about to be created. To do this he must pass from the competitive to the creative mind and harmonize with the Formless Intelligence — always creative, never competitive — through sincere gratitude. He must form a clear mental image of what he wishes to have, do, or become, contemplating it in leisure hours with unwavering faith and devout gratitude; the creative energy works through established channels of trade. To receive, he must act: more than fill his present place, doing each day's acts successfully, giving use value in excess of cash value, and conveying increase. Riches come in exact proportion to the definiteness of vision, the fixity of purpose, the steadiness of faith, and the depth of gratitude.",
    context:"The book's self-summary: the whole system — creed, gratitude, vision, faith, efficient action, and increase — restated as one page of instructions.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ch. 17", text:"There is a thinking stuff from which all things are made, and which, in its original state, permeates, penetrates, and fills the interspaces of the universe." },
      { ref:"Ch. 17", text:"Gratitude unifies the mind of man with the intelligence of Substance, so that man’s thoughts are received by the Formless." },
      { ref:"Ch. 17", text:"The men and women who practice the foregoing instructions will certainly get rich; and the riches they receive will be in exact proportion to the definiteness of their vision, the fixity of their purpose, the steadiness of their faith, and the depth of their gratitude." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Hold the creed, give thanks, contemplate the vision with faith, act efficiently each day, and convey increase — riches follow in proportion to all four.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"By the summary, in what proportion are riches received by those who practice the instructions?", choices:["In exact proportion to the definiteness of their vision, the fixity of their purpose, the steadiness of their faith, and the depth of their gratitude","In proportion to the hours they spend working","In proportion to their starting capital","Randomly, according to luck"], a:0, why:"The closing lines state that riches will be in exact proportion to the definiteness of vision, the fixity of purpose, the steadiness of faith, and the depth of gratitude." },
      { q:"Why does the summary insist man must pass from the competitive to the creative mind?", choices:["Because competition is illegal in most states","Because he otherwise cannot be in harmony with the Formless Intelligence, which is always creative and never competitive in spirit","Because competition exhausts the will","Because competitors pay higher taxes"], a:1, why:"Otherwise he cannot be in harmony with the Formless Intelligence, which is always creative and never competitive in spirit." },
      { q:"A reader wants a single daily practice tying the whole book together. The summary directs him to…", choices:["memorize the market reports","recruit friends to study with him","contemplate his Vision with unwavering faith and devout gratitude in his leisure hours, and do each day's acts in a successful manner","write out the creed once and file it away"], a:2, why:"Too much stress cannot be laid on frequent contemplation of the mental image with unwavering faith and devout gratitude; and he must do every day all that can be done that day, each act successful." }
    ] }
);
